Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Blackthorn Saplings Pierce the Clapper
- Story & Context: When the Syzygy Fair pitched its rusted tents over the salt-flats of Dungeness in 1999, Maren Vane wedged wild blackthorn branches inside twelve church-cast cowbells to mute their ring until the moon crossed the sun. As the corona flared into view, the sheer sonic blast from the cinderblock-stacked bass cabs pulverized the brittle thorns into a fine, bitter dust that coated the crowd's tongues. The band surged forward through eight minutes of grinding concrete drones before Maren screamed the carnival's open ledger into a broken bullet microphone: "Drink the black sun dry or spit the iron out!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: An extreme macro photograph of a rusted iron bell clapper split open by sharp, blooming blackthorn vines, set inside an abandoned concrete bunker lit only by an eerie, haloed solar corona.

Track 2: Rootstock (Or, The Grafting Chamber at Noon)
- Story & Context: Recorded inside the humid, cracked-glass atrium of the abandoned Orford Ness botanical conservatory, this confession piece chronicles percussionist Bram's exile from the traditional fairground circuit after he stole forty antique livestock bells to weld into a brutalist drone rack. The track captures the humid scent of damp peat and rotting orchids colliding with the screech of bowed iron rails, climaxing as Bram confessed to sabotaging his family's daylight calliope. Mid-crescendo, Bram's gravelled voice cracks through the feedback: "Cut the stem at midday and watch the marrow rot!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A cinematic still looking up through shattered Victorian greenhouse panes at an eclipsed black sun, tangled with creeping ivy and hanging rows of oxidized copper cowbells dripping with condensation.

Track 3: What Seed Survives the Occlusion?
- Story & Context: Local myth claims that during the 2015 total blackout over the North Sea, an offshore oil derrick answered the band's final twenty-minute post-rock crescendo with its own industrial foghorn, mistaking their brutalist bell ringing for a navigational catastrophe warning. Fishermen onshore reported smelling ozone and charred loam while the water turned oily and dead-still, right as the band smashed their iron bells against the shoreline boulders at the very second sunlight broke through. The collective chant was recorded by a lone hydrophone dropped fifty fathoms into the surf: "No green will crawl until the bell forgives the dark!"
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A vast, melancholic shoreline at total eclipse, showing monumental brutalist concrete sea-defenses littered with crushed iron bells and sprouting sea-kelp under a cold, coronal halo.

